    "refine.guide.shape.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Shapes: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Pick a figure among the tool’s options at the bottom, then drag across the box the figure should fill, from one corner to the opposite one. For a line, drag from one end to the other. The figure follows your finger until you lift it. Each time Refine opens, the bar starts on Line.\n\nA closed figure makes a new cell. Like a shape drawn with the [Pen](guide:pen), it keeps the paint of the cell it was drawn in until you paint it, unless Color from Photo is on.\n\nA curve takes two drags. The first sets its ends, and the second bends it toward your finger. To leave it straight, tap anywhere on the picture, or pick another tool. Undo takes the curve away. While a curve waits, a line under the tools reminds you: “Drag to bend the curve, or tap to keep it straight.”\n\nThe Snap switch works only on lines and curves: it moves their ends onto lines near them. Rectangles, ovals, hexagons and stars go exactly where you drag them, so draw them clear of other lines or well across them.\n\nA drag too small to make a figure does nothing.\n\n## On the practice picture\nDraw a star in the sky. Then pick Curve and draw a path over the near hill from the tree to the cottage’s door: drag its ends first, then drag again to bend it. Its ends land on the tree’s and the cottage’s lines, but the hill runs on beneath both, so the curve closes nothing off: it stays a line of ink, a path drawn on the hill."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.eraser.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Eraser: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Rubs out the ink it touches."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.eraser.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Eraser: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "The Eraser takes out ink, the lines you drew and the lines the app drew alike. Where it breaks a line between two cells, even by a small gap, those cells become one, with the paint and number of the larger cell. The rest of the line stays, and is still drawn on the finished picture, now inside one cell, so it can look as if nothing changed; turn on Numbers in [Layers](guide:layers) and you’ll see one number where there were two. If two cells joined that you wanted apart, undo, or close the gap again with the Pen.\n\nIt leaves the edge of the picture alone, and the dashed edges between cells, which have no ink; to take those away, use [Merge](guide:merge). It also erases the letters the app drew where your photo had writing.\n\nThe Size slider sets how wide the Eraser rubs on the screen, not on the picture, so it rubs finer when you zoom in. A tap rubs out one round spot the size of the eraser. With the Apple Pencil hovering over the picture, a ring shows how much it will take.\n\nIf erasing would leave a cell without room for its number, a note at the top says “That couldn’t be erased.” and nothing changes.\n\n## On the practice picture\nZoom in on the cottage’s window and tap the middle of its cross. The four panes become one, and most of the cross stays."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.smartEraser.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Line Eraser: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Takes out a whole line with one tap."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.smartEraser.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Line Eraser: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Tap a line and the Line Eraser takes out the stretch you tapped. It stops where three or more lines meet. Where one line just continues into another, it keeps going, so a tap can take more than you expect. A closed loop goes whole. The stretch flashes as it goes, and Undo brings it back.\n\nYour tap needs to land on or close to the line; a miss brings a note: “Tap a line to erase it.” A line you’ve only just drawn can’t be erased this way until it has settled into the picture, a moment later.\n\nDragging rubs out ink as the [Eraser](guide:eraser) does, at its Size. With either eraser, the cells the line kept apart become one, in the larger cell’s paint.\n\n## On the practice picture\nTap the line around the moon: it goes whole, and the moon takes the sky’s paint. Then tap the outline of the cottage’s door: the door joins the wall, in the wall’s paint."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.bendLine.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Bend Line: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Drag a line to reshape it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.bendLine.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Bend Line: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Put your finger on a line and drag. The spot you grabbed follows your finger, and the line curves smoothly on either side of it, bending less the farther it is from your finger. Its ends stay where they meet other lines, and the cells on both sides keep their paint and follow the new shape. You see where the line will go before you lift your finger.\n\nStart the drag on the line itself, within about 16 points of it, and away from where lines meet. A line grabbed right where it meets another won’t move. If your drag starts away from any line, a note at the top says “Start the drag on a line.”\n\nA line can’t bend across another line, off the picture, or so far that a cell has no room left for its number. If your drag would do that, the line stays where it was and the note says “That line couldn’t be moved there.” A shorter drag usually works, and you can drag the same line as many times as you like.\n\nBend Line moves the drawn lines. The dashed edges between cells, which have no ink, move with [Move Edge](guide:moveEdge). With this tool, touching and holding doesn’t pick up a color as it does with most others: you’re holding the line.\n\n## On the practice picture\nGrab the far hills’ line near the middle and pull it up into a mountain."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.select.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Select: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Shows a cell, its paint, and how many cells share it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.select.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Select: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Tap a cell to select it. It glows, and the bar at the bottom shows its paint’s number and name and how many areas, as the bar calls cells, use that paint. Tap it again, or tap off the picture, to let it go. Selecting a cell doesn’t change your current paint: the ring in the palette stays where it was.\n\nWhile any Cells tool is picked, dashed lines show the borders between cells where no line is drawn. They divide the paint, but they won’t be drawn on the finished picture.\n\nChange Color in the bar opens the cell’s card. If other cells share its paint, the card’s color picker gives this cell a color of its own and leaves the others as they are; it’s labelled Only This Area. If it’s the only cell with that paint, the picker is called Color and changes the paint itself. The swatches below it give the cell one of the picture’s other paints instead. If you pick a color very close to a paint the picture already has, the cell gets that paint.\n\nOn the card of a cell that shares its paint, Show Its Other Areas takes you to [Edit Paint](guide:editPaint) with that paint picked and its cells glowing.\n\nIf you recolor the last cell of a paint, that paint is removed, and the paints after it are renumbered.\n\n## On the practice picture\nSelect the moon and open its card: it’s the only cell of its paint, so the picker is Color. Then select one of the window’s panes, which share one yellow paint: its picker is Only This Area."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.cellEdge.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Cell Edge: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Divides a cell without drawing a line."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.cellEdge.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Cell Edge: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Cell Edge cuts a cell in two without drawing a line. The edge shows dashed while you work, and isn’t drawn on the finished picture. Both halves keep the paint they had, so all you’ll see is the dashed line and sparkles where the ends met; turn on Numbers in [Layers](guide:layers) to see each half’s number. Then give one half another paint with the [Bucket](guide:bucket) or the [Paint Brush](guide:brush).\n\nDraw from one edge of the cell to another, or draw a loop inside it. With Snap on, an end near a drawn line moves onto it, and an end that practically touches a dashed edge joins it too. If the edge closes nothing off, doubles back, or runs too close to another line, a note at the top says “That area couldn’t be divided there.”\n\nTo take an edge away again, drag across it with [Merge](guide:merge). Tapping a half with [Make Cell](guide:fill) also joins them again, since its fill spreads across dashed edges.\n\n## On the practice picture\nDraw a loop on the near hill, a pond, ending where you began. Then draw an edge across the far hills, from the line along their top down to the near hill’s line."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.merge.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Merge: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Joins the cells you drag across into one."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.merge.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Merge: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Start in the cell whose paint you want to keep, and drag across the cells that should join it. The first cell keeps its paint and number. The edges between them are removed, drawn lines as well as dashed ones. Once your finger has crossed into a second cell, the cells that will join light up.\n\nA cell joins only if it touches the cell you’re building. If your drag never leaves the first cell, a note at the top says “That’s already one area.” If none of the cells you crossed touch it, the note says “Those areas couldn’t be joined.”\n\nIf no cell is left using a paint, that paint is removed from the palette, and each paint after it moves down a number.\n\nTo divide a cell again, draw an edge with [Cell Edge](guide:cellEdge), or a line with the [Pen](guide:pen).\n\n## On the practice picture\nDrag across the window’s four panes to make one big pane: the cross goes too, since Merge takes away the lines between the cells it joins. Then drag from the sky into the moon: the moon disappears into the sky’s paint."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.moveEdge.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Move Edge: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Drag the border between two cells to change their shapes."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.moveEdge.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Move Edge: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Grab the border between two cells, inked or dashed, and drag it. One cell grows as the other shrinks, and both keep their paint. A border with ink takes its ink along.\n\nA border runs from one corner, where three or more borders meet, to the next. The corners stay put, and the border bends between them. Start your drag on the border itself, within about 16 points of it and away from the corners. If your drag starts away from any border, a note at the top says “Start the drag on the border between two cells.”\n\nA border can’t cross another, leave the picture, or squeeze a cell until its number no longer fits. If your drag would do one of those, nothing moves, and the note says “That line couldn’t be moved there.” Try a shorter drag.\n\nOn a border with ink, Move Edge and [Bend Line](guide:bendLine) do the same thing, so use either. Move Edge also moves the dashed ones, and Bend Line also moves a line that isn’t a border, such as a stroke inside a cell.\n\n## On the practice picture\nGrab the middle of the border between the two hills and drag it down a little, so the far hills grow taller."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.fill.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Make Cell: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Turns the shape you tap into a cell, in the photo’s color."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.fill.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Make Cell: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Tap inside a shape outlined in ink, and it becomes one cell, painted your photo’s color where you tapped. If that color is close to a paint the picture has, the cell takes that paint; otherwise a new paint is added. It’s the quickest way to color a shape as the photo has it, and it suits small shapes: a cell made this way may have a number about half the usual size, readable when zoomed in.\n\nThe fill spreads out to the ink around your tap, across any dashed edges (borders between cells that have no ink), and the cells it covers become one. If there’s no ink all the way around, and the fill reaches the edge of the picture, only the cell you tapped changes color.\n\nTap a cell that Make Cell made, and it goes back to how it was before. A tap on any other cell makes a new one.\n\nThe palette doesn’t show here, because the color comes from your photo. To pour a paint you’ve chosen, use the [Bucket](guide:bucket).\n\nIf there’s no room even for the smaller number, a note at the top says “That shape couldn’t be filled.” A shape that was too small for the Pen to make a cell isn’t closed off, so a tap inside it fills the cell around it instead.\n\n## On the practice picture\nThis page’s evening differs from the other pages’: the moon, the roof and the door are only outlined in ink, with the same paint as what’s around them. Tap inside the moon, then the roof and the door."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.bucket.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Bucket: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Pours the current paint into one cell."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.bucket.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Bucket: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Tap a cell and it takes the current paint, the one circled in the palette. Only that cell changes, even when other cells share its old paint. To change every cell of a paint at once, use [Edit Paint](guide:editPaint).\n\nPick the paint first: tap a swatch, touch and hold a spot on the picture to take its color, or choose any color with the color wheel at the end of the palette. A color the picture doesn’t have becomes a new paint, at the end of the palette, the first time you pour it. If you choose a color very close to a paint you already have, that paint is poured instead.\n\nChoose one new color and pour it before you choose the next: picking several new colors in a row keeps only the last.\n\nYou don’t need the Bucket to pour. With any tool that shows the palette, drag a swatch onto a cell.\n\n## When more changes color than you meant\nThe shape you poured into wasn’t closed. Its outline has a gap somewhere, so it was still part of the cell around it, and the paint filled that whole cell. Undo, close the gap with the [Pen](guide:pen) (two gold sparkles tell you a shape has closed), and pour again.\n\n## What else changes\nPouring into the last cell of a paint removes that paint from the palette, and every paint after it gets a number one lower. If the cell ends up beside a cell of the same paint and only a dashed edge lies between them, the two become one cell. A tap on a cell that already has the paint does nothing. If a cell has no room for its new number, a note at the top says “Those colors couldn’t be changed.”\n\nIf you color in Procreate, this is ColorDrop with your ink as the reference, and no threshold: a cell ends exactly at its edges, so paint spreads further only where an outline has a gap. Procreate is a trademark of Savage Interactive Pty Ltd, and this app isn’t connected with it.\n\n## On the practice picture\nThis page’s evening differs from the other pages’: the roof and the moon are only outlined, with the same paint as what’s around them. Choose a brick red with the color wheel and pour it into the roof. Then touch and hold the sky to pick up its paint, and tap the far hills to pour it in."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.brush.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Paint Brush: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Paints a stroke in the current paint."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.brush.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Paint Brush: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Brush across the picture, and everything under the stroke takes the current paint. Where the stroke crosses a cell, the part under it becomes a cell of its own, with dashed edges along the sides of the stroke. Where it touches a cell that already has the paint, the two join into one. It paints under the drawn lines and stops at them: a stroke across a line makes two cells, one on each side.\n\nStart moving as soon as you touch the screen. If your finger rests for half a second before it moves, Refine takes it as a touch and hold and picks up a color instead.\n\nThe Size slider sets the brush’s width on the screen, not on the picture, so zoom in to paint finer. A tap paints a single dab. With the Apple Pencil hovering over the picture, a ring shows the brush’s size.\n\nWhere the stroke would leave a sliver too small to hold a number, that sliver keeps its old paint. If the whole stroke is like that, nothing changes, and a note at the top says “That couldn’t be painted there.” A wider brush, or a stroke farther from the lines, usually works.\n\n## On the practice picture\nTouch and hold the cottage’s wall to pick up its cream, then paint a winding path over the near hill to the door."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.eyedropper.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Eyedropper: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Picks up a paint from the picture."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.eyedropper.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, the Eyedropper: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Tap a cell, and its paint becomes the current paint, circled in the palette.\n\nYou won’t often need to pick the Eyedropper itself. With most tools, touch and hold the picture for half a second, and a loupe appears: its top half is the color under your finger, its bottom half the current paint. Slide to the color you want and lift to pick it; your tool stays the same. Bend Line, Move Edge and Move Number are the exceptions: with them, you hold something to drag it.\n\nWhere your photo shows through more strongly than the paint, the Eyedropper picks the photo’s color. To pick from the photo, open [Layers](guide:layers), turn Photo on, and turn Paint below half or off. A color from the photo that matches no paint shows as a + swatch after the palette, and becomes a paint once you pour it.\n\n## On the practice picture\nTap the window to pick up its yellow. Then, in Layers, turn Photo on and Paint off, and pick up the peach glow above the hills."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.editPaint.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Edit Paint: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Changes a paint’s color everywhere it’s used."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.editPaint.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Edit Paint: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Edit Paint changes a paint, so every cell with its number changes together. Tap a cell or a swatch to pick a paint. Every cell with that paint glows. Then tap the color circle beside the paint’s number to choose its new color.\n\nThe Similar colors too switch is on to start with. It moves the paints near this one along with it: paints of about the same hue, or for a gray, the grays close to it. Turn it off to change one paint alone.\n\nDrag one swatch onto another to make them one paint, in the color of the one you drop it on. A note at the top says “Colors that came out alike share a number now.”\n\nTo recolor a single cell, use the [Bucket](guide:bucket), or select it with [Select](guide:select) and tap Change Color.\n\nChoosing one of the [Looks](guide:looks), or going back to the photo’s colors there, undoes every change made here.\n\n## On the practice picture\nPick the near hill and turn it an autumn orange; with Similar colors too on, watch the tree’s greens follow it. Then drag the window’s swatch onto the moon’s."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.looks.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Looks: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Recolors every paint at once, in a mood."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.looks.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Looks: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "A look moves every paint toward a time of day or a mood, while light parts stay light and dark parts dark: Moonlit, Golden Hour, Pastel, Garden or Starry Night. Once you’ve chosen one, Strength sets how far it goes. It starts at full each time you choose a look, and the picture changes when you let go of the slider. The Photo choice, beside the looks, goes back to your photo’s own colors.\n\nYou can have one look at a time. Choosing a look, or Photo, also takes away every change made with [Edit Paint](guide:editPaint), while colors you gave single cells, with the Bucket, the Paint Brush or Only This Area, stay. So choose your look first, then adjust single paints.\n\nWhen a look makes two paints too alike, they become one paint, and the note says “Colors that came out alike share a number now.”\n\n## On the practice picture\nTry each look on the evening scene, then bring Golden Hour’s Strength down to half."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.moveNumber.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Move Number: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Moves a number to a better spot in its cell."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.moveNumber.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Move Number: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "While Move Number is picked, the numbers show, whatever Layers says. Put your finger on a number and drag it where it’s easier to spot. It’s sized for the room where you leave it, but never smaller than a painter can read. At this zoom, numbers too small to read aren’t shown; zoom in and they appear.\n\nA number stays inside its own cell and off the lines. If you drop it where it has no room, or outside its cell, it goes back to where it was, and a note at the top says “There’s no room for that number there.” If your drag starts away from any number, the note says “Start the drag on a number.”\n\n## On the practice picture\nMove the sky’s number up beside the moon."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.text.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Writing: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Keeps the words in your photo readable."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.text.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Writing: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "When your photo has writing in it, the app looks for lines of text and draws their letters as fine pen lines, so they can still be read once the picture is painted. While you use Writing, each line it found has a box around it.\n\nEvery tap or drag here remakes the picture from your photo, which takes a moment, and then redoes your other changes on top. Any that no longer fit the new picture, such as a line that ended on a line that’s gone, are left out, and a note says how many.\n\nTap a box to turn that line off. The box turns dashed, and the writing is painted like the rest of the photo, which tends to lose the small parts of letters: an i’s dot, the inside of an O. Tap it again to turn it back on.\n\nIf the app missed some writing, drag a box over it from one corner to the opposite one. The box has to be a little taller than a line, so a flat swipe along the words does nothing. Tap a box you drew to take it away.\n\n## On the practice picture\nThe two lines written in ink on the paper and the jar’s label were found. The faint pencil line, “feed the cat”, wasn’t, so only pieces of it show. Drag a box over it, corner to corner, and wait for the picture to be remade. Then tap the box around HONEY and look at the O."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.tryPainting.summary" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Try Painting: one line under its name in the guide's list (iPhone)",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Paints the picture as it is now, to see how it feels."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.tryPainting.text" : {
      "comment" : "Tool Guide, Try Painting: the page's words. A paragraph per blank line; a line starting \"## \" is a heading in the reader's words, one starting \"- \" a list item; [name](guide:id) links another page (keep the guide:id). Quoted notes are Refine's own messages and the named controls its own labels: keep them as the app shows them",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Try Painting opens the painting screen on the picture with your changes, so you can check that its cells are pleasant to paint and its numbers easy to find. Paint a few cells as you would any painting, then close it to come back to Refine. Nothing you paint there is kept.\n\nIt shows the picture as it was the moment you tapped the button, so a change you made a split second before may not be in it.\n\n## On the practice picture\nDraw a cloud in the sky with the Pen, ending where you began so it becomes a cell, then tap Try Painting and paint the cloud and the sky around it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "refine.guide.open.hint" : {
